                <pb facs="0188" n="76" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg76"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77 Chinese Eastern/1386: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-03-12T19:00:00+09:00">March 12, 1935—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 12—10:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>52. The Soviet Embassy has confirmed the truth and accuracy of the Rengo
                    translation of the Foreign Office statement concerning the initialing of
                    documents relative to the sale of the Soviet rights in the Chinese Eastern
                    Railway to “Manchukuo”. The Soviet Embassy predicted that the formal signing
                    would take place on March 22 or 23 and stated that the whole agreement would
                    then be published.</p>
                <p>The spokesman of the Foreign Office has assured the Associated Press
                    representative that no specific mention of recognition of “Manchukuo” by the U.
                    S. S. R. occurs in any of the documents initialed yesterday.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
